{
  "version": 1,
  "note": "Canonical Category -> Subcategory taxonomy (stable domain model), the single source of truth for the validators AND the backend -- TaxonomyService loads this file at startup, so no Java service holds a hardcoded copy of the vocabulary any more. Navigation groups are NOT here -- they live in a separate presentation artifact (navigation.json), so the domain model stays stable when navigation changes or is later generated. This file is PURELY EDITORIAL: upstream provider vocabulary lives in source-mappings.json, loaded by the classification engine, so no vendor's words appear here. EDITORIAL CLASSIFICATION DRIVES NAVIGATION: a topic gathers CivicContent whose subcategory == the topic, and a category gathers CivicContent whose category_tags contain its label. Descriptive 'tags' are NEVER used to place content in the hierarchy -- they serve search, filtering and AI enrichment only, and cross-category relationships are an enrichment product persisted as metadata, not a second editorial taxonomy. 'matchCategoryTags' are the canonical editorial category_tags that associate any CivicContent item with it. Every source -- Resources, News, RSS/Laws, Flyers and Expert content -- classifies into THIS vocabulary; upstream label drift is normalized at the source, not compensated for by widening these lists (Decision 032).",
  "noticeKindsNote": "Controlled vocabulary for WHAT KIND of community notice an item is. It lives in `tags` on the record -- no new field, no new ContentType -- the same mechanism as the Seniors discovery tag, and squarely inside Decision 041's amendment: discovery paths may use controlled metadata when they represent a legitimate way residents find existing CivicContent. It is EDITORIAL, not presentational: a church posting a flyer knows whether it is a fundraiser or a public meeting, and this records what the producer already knows. NOTE there are three kinds, not four -- 'flyers' is not a kind, it is contentType, an axis that already exists. A flyer advertising a health fair carries kind 'event' and appears in BOTH the Events and Flyers views, because those are lenses over the same content rather than exclusive buckets. Applies to COMMUNITY-sector content; government content has its own destination. Enforced by validate_content_sources.py, which is the only script that resolves sector.",
